  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 452 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nThere isn't much to address in the long term period as long range \nguidance is in fairly decent agreement, indicating that high \npressure settles into the Mid-Mississippi Valley Monday, Tuesday, \nand possibly through Wednesday. Temperatures gradually warm through \nthe week, rising back to near/above normal.\n\nThe upper level trough that is giving rise to showers and \nthunderstorms today into the weekend will slowly meander through the \nTennessee/Ohio Valley Regions as elongated ridging builds in behind \nthe system. NBM blends have been a little generous with rain \nchances, skirting portions of the CWA Monday and Tuesday. Global \nguidance isn't favoring this as we get onto the anticyclonic side of \nthe upper level trough late Sunday into early Monday. This pattern \nseems to hold pretty well through Tuesday and even potentially into \nWednesday with surface high pressure parked under northwesterly mid \nand upper level flow. Monday and Tuesday look quite pleasant by \nAugust standards with highs in the 80s each afternoon.\n\nWarmth and moisture continue to slowly climb through late week with \nincreasing chance for rainfall sometime between late Wednesday and \ninto Thursday. Global guidance isn't too dispersed with subtle \ndifferences in the evolution of the upper level trough to the east \nand an approaching cold front from the north. Front timing is likely \nto adjust provided in extends well south of a Canadian low and \ninteracts with another surface low ejecting from the Plains. These \ntype of boundaries can often be influenced by increasing southerly \npush, causing it to slow or even wash out, depending on the \naggressiveness (or lack thereof) the warm push. Rain chances are most \nfavored at the tail end of the period, though specifics with timing \nand placement are likely to be fine tuned through the course of the \nweek.",
